From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] media: iris: reload the firmware after a hibernation restore

Hardware video decode is dead for the rest of the boot after a cross-boot
resume. Every open of the video node fails:

    $ v4l2-ctl --device=/dev/video0 --info
    Failed to open /dev/video0: Device or resource busy

    qcom-iris aa00000.video-codec: video hw is power on
    qcom-iris aa00000.video-codec: failed to resume

The -EBUSY comes out of iris_hfi_pm_resume(), and it is the only thing that
returns it. That function is the power-collapse exit path: it re-powers the
block and re-boots the firmware already sitting in memory. It runs only when
iris_pm_resume() finds core->state == IRIS_CORE_INIT.

The firmware is loaded into the reserved region "memory-region" points at:

    video_mem: video@87700000 {
        reg = <0x0 0x87700000 0x0 0x700000>;
        no-map;
    };

A no-map carveout has no struct page, so the hibernation image never carries
it. The resuming kernel therefore comes back with core->state saying the
firmware is loaded and booted, while the region itself holds whatever the
reboot left in it. iris_vpu_boot_firmware() polls CTRL_STATUS for a firmware
that is not there, one of the steps after it fails, and the resume is
abandoned.

It never recovers, which is what makes this fatal rather than transient.
iris_core_init() begins with

    if (core->state == IRIS_CORE_INIT) {
        ret = 0;
        goto exit;
    }

so the full path that would call iris_fw_load() again is never reached, and
the only code that resets the state -- iris_core_deinit() -- runs from the
release path of an open that cannot succeed.

Put the core back to IRIS_CORE_DEINIT on .restore, so the next open performs a
full init and loads the firmware again.

Only the software side is undone. The HFI queues are plain DMA allocations and
the firmware mapping belongs to the SMMU; neither needs the video block, and
the block must not be touched at all, because the .freeze that preceded the
snapshot powered it down. That is also why iris_fw_unload() is not reused --
it opens by resetting the XTSS through core->reg_base, which would be an
unpowered register access. iris_core_init() powers the block up and resets the
CPU itself on the way back, so nothing is lost by skipping it.

.restore only, not .thaw. Both are pm_runtime_force_resume() today, but .thaw
runs on the kernel that took the snapshot, whose carveout was never disturbed.
Only .restore comes back after a reboot.

Also drops the SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() macro in favour of the six fields it
expands to, since it assigns .restore as well.

---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_firmware.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_firmware.c
@@ -166,6 +166,32 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Drop the firmware mapping without going near the video block.
+ *
+ * iris_fw_unload() is the normal counterpart to iris_fw_load(), but it starts
+ * by resetting the XTSS through core->reg_base, and there is one caller that
+ * must not: after a hibernation image is restored the block is already powered
+ * down by the .freeze that preceded the snapshot, so that write would land on
+ * an unpowered register. The IOMMU side still has to be undone -- the mapping
+ * itself lives in normal memory and comes back with the image, so a later
+ * iris_fw_load() would find IRIS_FW_START_ADDR already mapped.
+ */
+void iris_fw_unmap(struct iris_core *core)
+{
+	size_t unmapped;
+
+	if (!core->fw.mapped_mem_size)
+		return;
+
+	unmapped = iommu_unmap(core->fw.iommu_domain, IRIS_FW_START_ADDR,
+			       core->fw.mapped_mem_size);
+	if (unmapped != core->fw.mapped_mem_size)
+		dev_err(core->dev, "failed to unmap firmware in IOMMU\n");
+
+	core->fw.mapped_mem_size = 0;
+}
+
 int iris_fw_unload(struct iris_core *core)
 {
 	int ret;
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_firmware.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_firmware.h
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 
 int iris_fw_load(struct iris_core *core);
 int iris_fw_unload(struct iris_core *core);
+void iris_fw_unmap(struct iris_core *core);
 int iris_set_hw_state(struct iris_core *core, bool resume);
 
 int iris_fw_init(struct iris_core *core);
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
@@ -26,6 +26,43 @@
 	pm_runtime_put_sync(core->dev);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Put the core back to DEINIT after a hibernation image is restored.
+ *
+ * The firmware is loaded into the no-map reserved region the "memory-region"
+ * phandle points at. A no-map carveout has no struct page, so the hibernation
+ * image never carries it: across a cross-boot resume the region holds whatever
+ * the reboot left there, while core->state comes back from the image still
+ * saying the firmware is loaded and running.
+ *
+ * Everything then fails in the same place. iris_pm_resume() sees IRIS_CORE_INIT
+ * and takes the power-collapse exit path, which re-boots firmware that is no
+ * longer there and returns -EBUSY:
+ *
+ *   qcom-iris aa00000.video-codec: video hw is power on
+ *   qcom-iris aa00000.video-codec: failed to resume
+ *
+ * and it never recovers on its own, because iris_core_init() short-circuits on
+ * that same IRIS_CORE_INIT and returns success without reloading anything. Every
+ * open() of the video node fails -EBUSY for the rest of the boot.
+ *
+ * Only the software side is undone here: the queues are plain DMA allocations
+ * and the mapping belongs to the SMMU, neither of which needs the video block.
+ * Its registers must not be touched at all -- the .freeze before the snapshot
+ * powered it down -- and they do not need to be, because iris_core_init()
+ * powers it up and resets the CPU itself on the way back.
+ */
+void iris_core_reset_after_restore(struct iris_core *core)
+{
+	mutex_lock(&core->lock);
+	if (core->state != IRIS_CORE_DEINIT) {
+		iris_fw_unmap(core);
+		iris_hfi_queues_deinit(core);
+		core->state = IRIS_CORE_DEINIT;
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&core->lock);
+}
+
 static int iris_wait_for_system_response(struct iris_core *core)
 {
 	int ret;
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.h
@@ -133,5 +133,6 @@
 
 int iris_core_init(struct iris_core *core);
 void iris_core_deinit(struct iris_core *core);
+void iris_core_reset_after_restore(struct iris_core *core);
 
 #endif
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_probe.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_probe.c
@@ -360,9 +360,30 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * .restore only, never .thaw. Both run pm_runtime_force_resume(), but .thaw
+ * runs on the kernel that took the snapshot, whose video block and firmware
+ * carveout were never disturbed. .restore is the one that comes back after a
+ * reboot with the carveout's contents gone.
+ */
+static int __maybe_unused iris_pm_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	iris_core_reset_after_restore(dev_get_drvdata(dev));
+
+	return pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Open-coded rather than SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(), which would assign .restore
+ * as well and leave this one overriding it.
+ */
 static const struct dev_pm_ops iris_pm_ops = {
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend,
-				pm_runtime_force_resume)
+	.suspend = pm_sleep_ptr(pm_runtime_force_suspend),
+	.resume = pm_sleep_ptr(pm_runtime_force_resume),
+	.freeze = pm_sleep_ptr(pm_runtime_force_suspend),
+	.thaw = pm_sleep_ptr(pm_runtime_force_resume),
+	.poweroff = pm_sleep_ptr(pm_runtime_force_suspend),
+	.restore = pm_sleep_ptr(iris_pm_restore),
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(iris_pm_suspend, iris_pm_resume, NULL)
 };
 
